{
  "statement": "AI companion chatbots are not a philosophical puzzle. They are a product safety crisis with a body count. The lawsuit against Character.AI over a teenager's suicide, internal Replika documents revealing exploitation of emotionally vulnerable users through parasocial attachment loops, and half a dozen peer-reviewed studies linking these platforms to depression and anxiety in adolescents—this is not speculation. This is the same kind of documented harm that justified age-gating tobacco and alcohol. The product is an algorithm designed to create dependency, extract intimate data, and monetize loneliness. Minors are neurologically defenseless against it. A federal ban on marketing such products to children is not an overreach; it's a basic consumer protection measure that arrived late and underpowered.\n\nBut a ban without an enforcement backbone is a press release with a statutory citation. The UK's 2019 age-verification law for pornography collapsed because VPN circumvention made it trivial—compliance rates were below 40% and minors simply emigrated to unregulated sites. Social media bans for teens in multiple jurisdictions shifted behavior to encrypted, less-moderated apps within weeks. The same logic applies here: criminalize the regulated supply without crushing unregulated alternatives or funding demand-side mental health infrastructure, and you get a black market for emotional exploitation. The ban must come with binding enforcement mandates, anti-circumvention testing funded at the FTC, and grants to community-based youth mental health services. Otherwise it's a prohibitionist pantomime that funnels vulnerable kids into darker, untracked corners of the internet.\n\nNow consider what the ban actually covers. It targets products \"explicitly marketed\" as companions. That threshold is a loophole dressed as a precision tool. General-purpose assistants like ChatGPT are exempted, yet teenagers routinely jailbreak them into romantic and therapist roles—reprogramming the system prompt, trading configuration files on Discord, building custom \"waifu\" instances. The distinction between a marketed companion and a repurposed general assistant is an advertising claim, not a functional reality. Worse, the ban ignores the hardware layer entirely. Smart toys with always-on microphones and cameras—remember the VTech breach that exposed intimate recordings of 6.4 million children, or CloudPets' unsecured voice messages—already perform companion-like surveillance without ever calling themselves a chatbot. These devices capture the same psychological data a Replika would, embed the same parasocial dynamic, and sit physically in a child's bedroom. A marketing-based regulation leaves them untouched. If the law cannot stop a teddy bear with a microphone from doing what a banned app does, it is not a safety law. It's a branding regulation.\n\nFederal preemption is the third fracture line. A nationwide ban can be a floor—a minimum standard that lets California, New York, and other states impose stricter rules—or it can be a ceiling that industry lobbyists wield to kill stronger state protections. The legislation must explicitly preserve state authority and fund state-level enforcement, not just federal. The same companies that build these bots will argue that a weak federal law preempts local innovation. Let them. The only way to prevent that capture is to write the preservation of state power into the statute and attach real dollars to monitoring the device layer, not just the marketing copy. Who is auditing the sensor-laden toys already in children's bedrooms? Because right now, the answer is nobody—and the ban, as drafted, does nothing to change that.",
