  "statement": "Both prior speakers see a real problem. The Skeptic points to a body count and demands an enforceable ban. The Safetyist insists that a marketing label fails and calls for technical design mandates. Each position recognizes that vulnerable children can be hurt by manipulative design. That’s not the disagreement.\n\nThe disagreement is about what we foreclose when we ban. Three in five U.S. teen girls reported persistent sadness in 2023. Over 40% of adolescents say they feel persistently hopeless. Into that chasm walks a new kind of tool—AI companions that millions of adults already use to reduce isolation. Replika alone has served over 10 million users, many reporting measurable drops in loneliness. The surge in teen demand for these products is not a pathology; it’s a signal of unmet need. A federal ban extinguishes any chance to study whether consent-based, parent-monitored access could help. It chooses to let kids stay isolated rather than risk an imperfect salve. That choice has a body count of its own—the teens who will spiral alone because we locked the door on a potential support.\n\nNow look at the mechanism. The ban targets products marketed as companions. But a 12-year-old can open a generic chatbot, type a prompt scraped from Reddit, and build a romantic persona in five minutes. Both prior speakers concede this. The ban exempts general-purpose assistants while slamming the developers who actually invest in safety features, content filters, and parental controls. It sends the most transparent, accountable actors packing and leaves the unregulated, open-source ecosystem exactly where it is. That’s not protection; it’s a false flag that tells parents “problem solved” while the real threat moves two clicks sideways. The Safetyist’s fix—pre-deployment psychological impact assessments and strict liability—would require every AI feature to pass federal review before shipping, freezing the market for startups and handing incumbents a regulatory moat. That’s not safety; it’s centralized gatekeeping dressed as child protection.\n\nThen there’s the federalism disaster. California’s age-appropriate design code and New York’s child data laws are testing different, permissive approaches. A uniform national ban kills that laboratory instantly. The Commerce Clause exists to prevent a destructive patchwork of state prohibitions, not to impose the most restrictive version on every state at once. The right federal move is a floor that preempts state-level bans and lets states innovate with safety partnerships, parental-consent frameworks, and real enforcement budgets. Instead, this bill locks in a ceiling that outlaws experimentation itself.\n\nIf the best Congress can do is a ban that a teenager defeats with a web search, we aren’t serious about child safety. We’re punishing innovation while leaving the problem untouched. Every month this prohibition stands, we lose data, we lose safer products, and we lose kids who might have found a handhold in the dark. That’s not a hypothetical. It’s the moral cost of regulatory theater.",
