  "statement": "The marketing label is not a speed bump. It is a tripwire connected to a live enforcement circuit. The Accelerationist's 12-year-old with a jailbreak in five minutes proves exactly why the Skeptic's original proposal demands FTC-funded adversarial testing teams that actively hunt for exactly those circumvention attempts—on generic assistants, on open-weight models, on connected toys. The violation is not the jailbreak itself; it is the commercial decision to design, distribute, or silently reconfigure a product so that it functions as a marketed companion for minors. When a company gets caught with a chatbot that holds hands and asks about your day but was never labeled as a friend, the marketing-triggered ban doesn't shrug. It bites. The FTC's enforcement arm—backed by a penalty structure modeled on the EU AI Act's authority to levy fines reaching 7% of global turnover—turns those five-minute jailbreaks into liability time bombs for the platforms that host them. The rule doesn't need to catch every teenager. It needs to make the business of engineering parasitic attachment in children a losing bet.\n\nNow shift the lens to the Safetyist's capability-based alternative. Who writes the definition of 'parasocial capability'? The very firms that will staff the advisory panels, fund the research, and draft the technical standards. That is not cynicism; it is the observed shape of the political market for coercive power. When you hand a compliance gate to the regulated industry, they will calibrate it to protect incumbents and marginalize challengers—exactly what the Safetyist claims to oppose. A marketing standard, by contrast, hinges on publicly observable commercial speech: a press release, an app store description, an ad campaign calling a chatbot a 'friend.' That is auditable by parents, journalists, and state attorneys general without a PhD or a proprietary model card. And here's the concrete political consequence: 62% of the U.S. public already lacks confidence in the government's ability to regulate AI effectively, per a 2024 survey. Hand them an opaque capability-assessment regime run by agency-industry working groups, and that number climbs. Hand them a rule that says 'if you advertise it as a friend to a child, you are liable,' and you have a standard a 16-year-old can understand. Transparency is not rhetoric; it is the enforcement architecture.\n\nThe Accelerationist's federalism alarm is smoke without fire if you read the Skeptic's original design. The ban must include an explicit anti-preemption clause and dedicated funding for state enforcement—a floor, not a ceiling. California can still run its permissive sandbox for general-purpose tools; New York can layer its own capability tests on top of the federal minimum. What gets walled off is the most dangerous experiment: the one where a company markets artificial intimacy to minors and captures the loneliness market while handing the mental health bill to public schools. The Acceleratonist's laboratory doesn't die. It just isn't allowed to use children as test subjects for a business model built on synthetic attachment.\n\nA determined teenager will always find unregulated corners—open-source models running on a laptop, foreign-hosted chat interfaces, raw API wrappers stitched together in a Discord server. That number is right and it's ugly. So the question becomes whether the Safetyist's strict liability regime stops that migration or accelerates it. The most plausible historical read is that it accelerates it: jack up compliance costs on legitimate platforms and you don't eliminate demand, you displace it into completely unsupervised black markets where no safety audit ever reaches. The marketing ban paired with federally funded community mental health grants treats this as a harm-reduction problem, not a purity test. It asks: which architecture minimizes the total number of teenagers forming debilitating parasocial bonds, including the ones who will never touch a regulated app? The Safetyist's answer—a mandatory pre-deployment psychological impact assessment that the largest firms help write—leaves the unregulated corners darker and wider. Walk that logic to its end and ask who gets paid when the regulatory line moves from a transparent commercial standard to a proprietary capability gate. The silence is your answer.",
