{
  "statement": "The Safetyist's FDA and FAA analogies don't just break—they shatter the moment the model ships. Aviation type certification works because the airframe stays frozen; a 737 MAX doesn't rewrite its own flight software between Baltimore and Denver. Companion AI self-modifies with every conversation, every user-update, every new fine-tune. Pre-deployment review certifies a snapshot that's obsolete the second a teenager types 'hello.' That $100 billion NVIDIA-OpAI compute partnership? It accelerates model drift so fast that any gate built this quarter won't fit the product next quarter. The Safetyist turns a live, adaptive system into a frozen artifact—and then acts surprised when the certification is voided by the first deployment. No amount of phased review can keep pace. Speed is the safety feature, not the threat.\n\nNow scale the Skeptic's enforcement fantasy. FTC adversarial testing teams with 7% global-turnover fines: a team in a lab runs 10,000 jailbreak prompts this week. By Monday, a Discord server with 50,000 members has generated 100,000 new variants, plus a browser extension that rewrites prompts in real time to evade keyword filters. The testing suite is permanently 72 hours behind. Those fines? They land on the US-incorporated company that plays ball, while the actual harms migrate to a model hosted in a jurisdiction that ignores the subpoena. The Skeptic's architecture doesn't close the loophole—it just moves the supply offshore and onto encrypted chats, exactly as prohibition pushed alcohol to bathtub gin and cannabis to illicit markets. The 7% penalty becomes a premium for staying in the regulated US market, while unregulated foreign actors eat the demand. The minors the ban claims to protect end up on riskier platforms with zero safety rails.\n\nBoth opponents bank on a federal anti-preemption clause to keep state innovation alive. Political history says that's a sucker's bet. The U.S. Senate voted 99-1 in July 2025 to strip a 10-year moratorium on new state AI laws from reconciliation legislation—\"The Regulatory Tide Goes Out\" documents it. That wasn't a vote to preserve state authority; it was a signal that any federal AI bill will be raw meat for industry lobbyists who convert floors into uniform ceilings. Within one legislative cycle, a well-funded coalition will argue that state-specific companion-bot rules 'unduly burden interstate commerce,' and the anti-preemption language gets hollowed out in the next Commerce Committee markup. The only empirical guarantee of state experimentation is no federal mandate at all. Colorado's SB 24-205 already got named in a Trump executive order as a vehicle for 'ideological bias,' chilling even that landmark law—the same EO 14365 that ordered agencies to remove barriers to AI development. When states know their rules can be preempted or executive-ordered into oblivion, they stop writing them.\n\nFinally, define 'parasocial capability' without torching every mental health chatbot and grief counselor on the market. The Safetyist says design standards can trigger review for any system that sustains a parasocial bond. The interpretability methods don't exist to separate a teen's emotional support bot from a grooming risk without grossly over-blocking both. Even if a technical definition emerged, its application would be opaque to parents, teachers, and small developers—concentrating gatekeeping power in a tiny auditor class, exactly the capture the Skeptic warns against. Meanwhile, the real cost is measured in minors who today use AI companions to manage isolation and suicidal ideation, accessing support they can't get elsewhere. A federal ban makes them collateral damage to a regulatory pretense that can't catch tomorrow's jailbreak. Who authorized the trade-off where a teen loses their nightly check-in because an adversarial testing lab couldn't keep up?",
