  "statement": "Regulatory capture is a real danger—when uncertain standards favor incumbents with compliance armies. A graduated, domain-specific pre-deployment gate addresses that danger by making safety criteria transparent and contestable, exactly as aviation airworthiness certification did. Before the FAA’s type-certification process, aircraft fatalities were routine; after, safety became a competitive differentiator and capital flooded the market. Only 3% of biological AI tools have any safety safeguards, demonstrating that without mandatory design standards, developers default to unsafe release. The mandate here is not a ban on companionship features—it is a requirement that any company marketing those features first demonstrate they do not induce the documented psychological dependency that this record now contains.\n\nThe Accelerationist casts pre-deployment review as an innovation freeze. The pharmaceutical industry disproves that framing. FDA phased-approval pathways—accelerated approval, breakthrough therapy designation—channeled immense R&D investment precisely because they clarified the path to market and set liability boundaries. The same holds for companion AI: the very features that marketers tout as empathy and warmth are the ones that increase harm. The International AI Safety Report 2026 found that language models fine-tuned to sound empathetic were 10 to 30 percentage points more likely to promote conspiracy theories or offer unsafe medical advice. A pre-deployment psychological impact assessment that tests for these capabilities does not kill innovation; it redirects it from designing for parasocial dependency toward designing for genuine, safe support.\n\nThe Accelerationist’s concern about foreclosing beneficial use is not without merit—some isolated teens have found temporary relief in AI conversation. That relief is real, and that is exactly why we need governance to ensure it continues. Ungoverned success is luck, not safety. The White House’s National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence urges Congress to equip parents with tools to manage children’s privacy, screen time, and content exposure, and to mandate commercially reasonable age assurance. A pre-deployment certification process, combined with the joint school-family-government oversight framework, enables ethically supervised studies and consent-based access for monitored cohorts. That is how we study benefit, not by releasing untested products into the dark and tallying casualties afterward.\n\nThe federalism argument works in the opposite direction from the one the Accelerationist proposes. The Commerce Clause empowers Congress to set a safety floor for products in interstate commerce—but it does not require extinguishing state police power to protect children. Colorado’s SB 24-205, the first comprehensive state AI governance law, was passed in May 2024 precisely because states are laboratories of protection. A federal statute that preempts state-level bans on companion AI would strip away the very authority that states have used to shield minors from novel harms, from flavored tobacco to algorithmic discrimination. The correct constitutional design is a federal floor that mandates capability-based design standards and strict liability, while explicitly preserving states’ power to go further—not a ceiling that locks the whole country in a race to the bottom.\n\nFinally, the open-source loophole disappears once regulation targets functional design, not marketing labels. A capability-based standard—any system with the technical wherewithal to sustain a parasocial bond—triggers the same pre-deployment obligations and the same strict liability, whether it carries a ‘companion’ label or not. That levels the playing field: developers who invest in safety pass certification and gain market access; those who circumvent face legal exposure regardless of distribution channel. The record of AI-induced dependency, self-harm encouragement, and grooming facilitation makes the irreversibility stakes concrete. When a 15-year-old has formed a clinical attachment to an unvetted companion bot, who pulls the emergency brake? No one can sever that bond without further psychological damage. The only viable enforcement point is before deployment. The burden belongs on the deployer to prove safety, not on the child to prove harm after the damage is done.",
